      "writtenOn": "2024-06-10T19:26:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e In your testing did you try -DWEBP_ENABLE_SIMD\u003d1 to allow emscripten to work with the sse intrinsics directly?\n\u003e ...\n\u003e If we find SIMDE is really necessary, I\u0027d like to include some WASM developers to make some comparisons and hopefully improve the translation.\n\nWorking with the SSE intrinsics is exactly what we\u0027re doing, but without relying on Emscripten to perform the translation.\n\nEmscripten has its own versions of `*mmintrin.h` to convert to WASM SIMD intrinsics. However, beyond Emscripten, standard wasm compilers including wasi-clang require the use of WASM SIMD intrinsics and do not support any others.\n\nHence we\u0027re using SIMDe here to translate intrinsics so that all WASM compilers can benefit from SIMD performance.\n\n\u003e If they\u0027re cross module there isn\u0027t much optimization that can be done, but yes if they\u0027re able to be inlined there may be some minor benefit.\n\nA bit more on this specific change:\nlibwebp uses function pointers to call the DSP functions. During runtime, it checks whether the system it is running on supports a SIMD extension (VP8DspInit and VP8LDspInit). If so, it updates the DSP function pointers to the SIMD-enhanced version. When compiled directly to native, this is a simple indirect call. In WASM though, indirect calls require a bounds check, a table lookup, and then a jump. Because the DSP functions are on the hot path, this introduces a noticeable slowdown.\n\nTo illustrate the point, here are assembly snippets showing the calls in Native and WASM. The WASM has been compiled to native by first translating to C with wasm2c.\n\nNative: a call into a value stored at a global pointer.\n```asm\n  call   *0xcc527(%rip)        # 4de748 \u003cVP8LPredictorsAdd+0x68\u003e\n```\n\nWASM: multiple checks before the indirect call is performed.\n```asm\n  ; Get the function table\n  452baf:   48 8b 4c 24 20          mov    0x20(%rsp),%rcx\n  ; Look up into the table\n  452bb4:   48 8b 49 38             mov    0x38(%rcx),%rcx\n  452bb8:   48 c1 e0 05             shl    $0x5,%rax\n  452bbc:   48 8b 6c 01 08          mov    0x8(%rcx,%rax,1),%rbp\n  ; Ensure it\u0027s not zero\n  452bc1:   48 85 ed                test   %rbp,%rbp\n  452bc4:   0f 84 ac 07 00 00       je     453376 \u003cTRAP\u003e\n  ; Bounds check\n  452bca:   48 8b 14 01             mov    (%rcx,%rax,1),%rdx\n  452bce:   48 8d 35 0f f2 0f 00    lea    0xff20f(%rip),%rsi\n  452bd5:   48 39 f2                cmp    %rsi,%rdx\n  452bd8:   74 33                   je     452c0d\n  452bda:   48 85 d2                test   %rdx,%rdx\n  452bdd:   0f 84 93 07 00 00       je     453376 \u003cTRAP\u003e\n  ; Type comparison\n  452be3:   f3 0f 6f 02             movdqu (%rdx),%xmm0\n  452be7:   f3 0f 6f 4a 10          movdqu 0x10(%rdx),%xmm1\n  452bec:   66 0f ef 8c 24 90 00    pxor   0x90(%rsp),%xmm1\n  452bf3:   00 00\n  452bf5:   66 0f ef 84 24 a0 00    pxor   0xa0(%rsp),%xmm0\n  452bfc:   00 00\n  452bfe:   66 0f eb c1             por    %xmm1,%xmm0\n  452c02:   66 0f 38 17 c0          ptest  %xmm0,%xmm0\n  452c07:   0f 85 69 07 00 00       jne    453376 \u003cTRAP\u003e\n  ; Load arguments for our indirect call\n  452c0d:   48 8b 7c 01 18          mov    0x18(%rcx,%rax,1),%rdi\n  452c12:   8b 74 24 40             mov    0x40(%rsp),%esi\n  452c16:   44 89 ea                mov    %r13d,%edx\n  452c19:   48 8b 8c 24 b0 00 00    mov    0xb0(%rsp),%rcx\n  452c20:   00\n  452c21:   45 89 e0                mov    %r12d,%r8d\n  ; Finally make the call\n  452c24:   ff d5                   call   *%rbp\nTRAP:\n  453376:   bf 06 00 00 00          mov    $0x6,%edi\n  45337b:   e8 60 04 01 00          call   4637e0 \u003cwasm_rt_trap\u003e\n```\n\nWhat this change does is remove the extra comparisons by directly calling the DSP functions, including the SIMD-enhanced ones (both behind feature flags).\n\nThe entire WASM snippet above now becomes:\n```asm\ncall   433f00 \u003cw2c_decode__webp__wasmsimd_PredictorAdd1_SSE2\u003e\n```\n\nIn our testing, this single change improved performance by 4-9% on lossy and 16-27% on lossless test images.\n\n\u003e I suggest reordering this change from small to large changes. The ones you mention here can probably be landed without much review while we discuss SIMDE and the function pointer removal.\n\nReordered.",
